 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "THE WITCH OF WOOKEY HOLE. A stalagmite said to be a witch turned to stone by a monk. A local legend. The monk is not named. The witch is a minor celebrity."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "THE RIVER AXE. Rain that fell on the Mendips seeps for days through limestone before emerging here, at a constant 11 degrees. Paper mills used the water from 1610."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "wookey_mendip_water",
   "title": "Mendip Water",
   "text": "Rain that falls on the Mendip plateau sinks into the limestone and spends days underground before it comes out here as the River Axe. Wookey is a cave the river made, and the river is still at it."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 3,
   "plaque": "wookey_witch",
   "title": "The Witch of Wookey",
   "text": "THE WITCH OF WOOKEY. The legend says a witch lived in the cave until a monk from Glastonbury sprinkled holy water on her and turned her to stone. The stalagmite called the Witch is the evidence. The guide calls the monk Father Bernard. Nobody ever asked the witch her name."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "wookey_hyena_den",
   "title": "Ice Age Hyenas",
   "text": "HYENA DEN. Bones of hyenas and other Ice Age animals were dug out of Mendip caves like this one by Victorian scholars. Hyenas denned in the hills when the climate was far colder, and dragged their dinners home."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "A brass lever set into the rock by the water's edge. Someone has stencilled 'HATCH' on it in 1950s paint."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "A narrow stair behind an iron gate, with a handwritten card: 'Staff and Brave Visitors Only.'"
  }
 ],
